## Key Ideas

### Euclidean Distance

KNN defines similarity through **Euclidean distance** between feature vectors:

$$d(p, q) = \sqrt{(p - q)^\top (p - q)}$$

All 13 features contribute equally to this distance, so the relative scale of each feature matters. Larger-magnitude features (e.g. proline ≈ 278–1680) would dominate the distance calculation over smaller ones (e.g. ash ≈ 1.4–3.2) if the features were not already on comparable scales in this dataset.

---

### The KNN Algorithm

For each query point:

1. Compute the distance to every training point.
2. Sort training points by distance (ascending).
3. Take the $k$ closest.
4. Return the **majority label** (classification) or **mean** (regression).

There is no training phase — the model *is* the data. All computation happens at prediction time, which makes KNN memory-efficient to "train" but slow at prediction for large datasets.

---

### Classification Error

$$\text{Error}(k) = \frac{\text{number of misclassifications}}{n_{\text{test}}}$$

The complement, accuracy = $1 - \text{Error}$, is reported after the optimal $k$ is selected.

---

### Choosing $k$

The $k$ sweep (Section 9) illustrates the bias–variance trade-off directly:

| $k$ | Effect |
|:----|:-------|
| Small (e.g. $k=1$) | Low bias, high variance — prediction changes dramatically with small data shifts |
| Large (e.g. $k=25$) | High bias, low variance — decision boundary over-smoothed, misses local structure |
| Optimal | Minimum test error; found by plotting error vs. $k$ and identifying the elbow |

The notebook sweeps only **odd** values to avoid ties in majority voting.

